  <section class="content-section reveal">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 lg:px-6">
      <div class="reveal grid-2">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Operational scenarios</p>
          <h2 class="section-title">Where sovereign edge computing delivers</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose-content">
          <p>Defence organisations across the MEA region face operational scenarios that commercial infrastructure cannot address. MEAICON's platforms are engineered for three mission-critical use cases that define modern defence computing.</p>
          <p><strong>Sovereign edge computing in denied environments.</strong> Forward-deployed units operating in contested terrain — desert borders, coastal approaches, and remote intelligence collection points — cannot depend on connectivity to central data centres. MEAICON deploys edge computing nodes that process intelligence data locally, maintain operational pictures during connectivity denial, and queue zeroization commands for deferred execution when satellite links are restored. Every node operates autonomously for up to 30 days without resupply, maintaining mission continuity regardless of network conditions.</p>
          <p><strong>Secure tactical communications.</strong> Military communications across the Gulf and North African operational zones face interception, jamming, and traffic analysis threats. MEAICON's secure tactical communication platforms use hardware-enforced encryption, post-quantum-ready cryptography, and frequency-agile radio integration to maintain command and control integrity. Communications are encrypted at the silicon level — even if a tactical device is captured, the cryptographic material cannot be extracted through physical access.</p>
          <p><strong>Hardware-level data protection for captured devices.</strong> In denied environments, computing devices may fall into adversarial hands. MEAICON's platforms enforce security at the hardware level through TPM 2.0 root of trust, secure boot chains, anti-tamper sensors, and remote zeroization. When a device detects physical intrusion, cryptographic data destruction initiates before extraction is possible. When headquarters detects compromise, remote zeroization commands execute across the fleet regardless of individual node connectivity status.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

